<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- NuGet packaging metadata -->
    <Description>Azure Key Vault Keys implementation of SharedKernel.Cryptography's IEncryptionKeyProvider and IEnvelopeEncryptionProvider. AzureKeyVaultEncryptionKeyProvider's direct-retrieval mode (IEncryptionKeyProvider) is built internally on top of its own envelope-wrap mode (IEnvelopeEncryptionProvider, backed by CryptographyClient.WrapKeyAsync/UnwrapKeyAsync) — Azure Key Vault Keys does not export raw HSM-protected key material by default, so the vault's own master key material never crosses the process boundary either way. Fails closed: any Azure SDK exception propagates directly, never a silent fallback. The one 01.Core package with a genuine third-party vendor SDK dependency (Azure.Security.KeyVault.Keys + Azure.Identity), kept out of the zero-third-party-dependency SharedKernel.Cryptography core — mirrors why SharedKernel.Storage.S3/.Obs are separate packages from SharedKernel.Storage.Abstractions. Shipped (P-494/WO-081, additive): a new, deliberately separate class, AzureKeyVaultAsymmetricKeyProvider, implements the async IAsymmetricKeyProvider (P-493) for REMOTE RSA/ECDSA sign/verify — GetRsaKeyAsync/GetEcdsaKeyAsync return a thin RSA/ECDsa subclass whose SignHash/VerifyHash overrides (the real overridable BCL extension points, since SignData/VerifyData are non-virtual) delegate to Azure Key Vault's genuine synchronous CryptographyClient.Sign/Verify calls, mapping the fixed SHA-256+PSS / SHA-256-on-P-256 combinations RsaSignatureService/EcdsaSignatureService already use to Azure's PS256/ES256 algorithms. Zero changes to RsaSignatureService/EcdsaSignatureService were needed. Never exports private key material (ExportParameters/ImportParameters always throw); never implements ISynchronousAsymmetricKeyProvider (every call is a real, unavoidable blocking Key Vault network round trip); caches one CryptographyClient per distinct Azure key name from its first implementation, never per call. Shipped (P-496/WO-081, additive): AzureKeyVaultEncryptionKeyProvider now reuses one CryptographyClient per resolved (Azure key name, version) pair instead of constructing one per call, and replaces its old self-decodable ~470-byte envelope CryptographicKey.Id with a short, durable, Key-Vault-Secrets-backed version tag ("v1", "v2", ...) shared across every replica via a single "current version" pointer secret — fixing a real, previously-undocumented defect where every process/pod independently minted its own unique data key with no cross-replica sharing. A new explicit MintNewVersionAsync(CancellationToken) operational method (never part of IEncryptionKeyProvider/IEnvelopeEncryptionProvider) mints a fresh version deliberately — GetCurrentKeyAsync never auto-mints one. GetKeyAsync stays backward-read-compatible with the pre-P-496 envelope shape indefinitely — no forced data migration, no breaking change. Pulls in a second Azure SDK family, Azure.Security.KeyVault.Secrets, confined to this package.</Description>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>WO-083 / P-511 (fix, no signature changes): fixed the identical cross-caller cancellation-token leak found in SharedKernel.Cryptography's CachedEncryptionKeyProvider (see that package's own release notes) at three sites in this package: AzureKeyVaultEncryptionKeyProvider's two internal caches (_resolvedKeysByCacheKey, _plaintextKeysByTag) and AzureKeyVaultAsymmetricKeyProvider's _resolvedKeysByAzureKeyName. All three now share a common internal SingleFlightCache&lt;TKey,TValue&gt; helper applying the identical fix: a cache-slot-owned CancellationTokenSource (never any individual caller's token) drives the shared Key Vault call, each caller awaits via Task.WaitAsync(callerCt) so one caller's own cancellation never disturbs another caller's concurrent request for the same key/tag, and an abandoned-or-failed slot is evicted immediately once every waiter has departed so a future caller never joins an already-doomed resolution. AzureKeyVaultAsymmetricKeyProvider additionally gained a new internal test-seam constructor (mirroring AzureKeyVaultEncryptionKeyProvider's existing one) purely to make this fix independently verifiable without a reachable Azure Key Vault — production DI wiring is unaffected, always resolving the public single-parameter constructor.</PackageReleaseNotes>
    <PackageTags>shared-kernel;cryptography;azure;key-vault;keyvault;kms;envelope-encryption;encryption-key-provider;asymmetric-signing;rsa;ecdsa;remote-signing;key-rotation</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Azure.Security.KeyVault.Keys" />
    <PackageReference Include="Azure.Security.KeyVault.Secrets" />
    <PackageReference Include="Azure.Identity" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\SharedKernel.Cryptography\SharedKernel.Cryptography.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\SharedKernel.Configuration\SharedKernel.Configuration.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Exposes AzureKeyVaultEncryptionKeyProvider's internal test-seam constructor ONLY —
         production DI wiring always resolves the public two-parameter constructor (see that
         constructor's own remarks). Enables T-73's call-counting verification of the P-496
         connection-reuse/durable-registry behavior without a reachable Azure Key Vault. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="SharedKernel.Cryptography.KeyVault.Azure.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
